(3629420, 505602, 53316, '<td valign="top">Awesome colors.</td>', 'Awesome colors.', '2007-05-08 17:24:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(3629422, 501980, 67258, '<td valign="top">Have I just unwittingly discovered the mystery of peculiarly low scores?  I returned to this photo as a result of a mention in one of the forum threads, and could ''remember'' giving this a 7; I certainly <b>thought</b> 7, but for some reason I have pressed the 4 button.....  Apologies, and remain content in the knowledge that your score should really have been .001 (ok, I haven''t done the arithmetic) higher!</td>', 'Have I just unwittingly discovered the mystery of peculiarly low scores?  I returned to this photo as a result of a mention in one of the forum threads, and could ''remember'' giving this a 7; I certainly thought 7, but for some reason I have pressed the 4 button.....  Apologies, and remain content in the knowledge that your score should really have been .001 (ok, I haven''t done the arithmetic) higher!', '2007-05-08 17:25:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(3629423, 500047, 53316, '<td valign="top">So cute!!</td>', 'So cute!!', '2007-05-08 17:25:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(3629471, 505240, 30982, '<td valign="top">This is a beautiful view!  I saw in the Under 5 thread that you mentioned it was a bit bright and you didn''t know how to fix it.  One way (the best way) is to underexpose to begin with.  When shooting night shots, I''ve found you have to shoot at less than what the camera tells you.  Here you shot at 15 seconds.  In this same situation, try 10 seconds, maybe even 5.  <br/> <br/>In post processing, you can try reducing exposure, or using a duplicate layer with a blending mode set to multiply.  <br/> <br/>If none of this makes sense, send me a PM and I''ll try to explain further.  And congrats on a new personal best!</td>', 'This is a beautiful view!  I saw in the Under 5 thread that you mentioned it was a bit bright and you didn''t know how to fix it.  One way (the best way) is to underexpose to begin with.  When shooting night shots, I''ve found you have to shoot at less than what the camera tells you.  Here you shot at 15 seconds.  In this same situation, try 10 seconds, maybe even 5.   In post processing, you can try reducing exposure, or using a duplicate layer with a blending mode set to multiply.   If none of this makes sense, send me a PM and I''ll try to explain further.  And congrats on a new personal best!', '2007-05-08 17:37:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(3629486, 499192, 61651, '<td valign="top">Cool perspective - does that pier ever really end?</td>', 'Cool perspective - does that pier ever really end?', '2007-05-08 17:45:19.000000', NULL, 0),
